The '''Emperor of Nakamizu''' is the hereditary monarch and head of state of the [[Nakamizu]]. Under the current constitutional framework established following the [[Heisei Restoration]] of 1960, the emperor serves as "the symbol of the state and unity of the people" while executive authority is exercised by the elected government and parliament. The emperor remains the ceremonial head of the [[Imperial House of Nakamizu]] and performs cultural, diplomatic, and constitutional functions.

The emperor is traditionally referred to as the '''''Tennō''''' (天皇), meaning "Heavenly Sovereign", and is formally addressed as ''His Imperial Majesty''. In historical and literary contexts, the title '''Mikado''' (帝) is also occasionally used, particularly in foreign writings concerning Nakamizu during the imperial era.

The current imperial line traces its origin to the [[Kuji Restoration]] of 1803, when [[Emperor Kuji]] unified the imperial court and the shogunate under the consolidated Imperial–Kawaei dynasty following the [[Miyami Rebellion]]. Although the imperial family claims descent from the ancient Sanese emperors of [[Miyagi]], the modern Nakamizuan monarchy developed separately following the [[Schism of the Dual Suns]] in 1335, which permanently divided the northern and southern imperial branches that later became [[Okaiken]] and Nakamizu respectively.

The current emperor is [[Emperor Shinmyō|Emperor Shinmyō]] (Toshihito), who acceded to the [[Golden Chrysanthemum Throne]] in 1998 following the death of his father, [[Emperor Heisei]], marking the beginning of the [[Shinmyō]] era.

Historically, the role of the emperor fluctuated between direct political authority and symbolic religious leadership. Prior to the nineteenth century, political power in Nakamizu was frequently shared between the imperial court and powerful military governments headed by shoguns. This dual structure ended during the reign of Emperor Kuji, whose reforms centralized authority under the imperial state and laid the foundation for the modern Empire of Nakamizu.

Following the political crisis of the early twentieth century and the enactment of the [[Taihaku Act of 1903]], the monarchy entered the Commonwealth period, during which the emperor exercised little direct authority. Limited constitutional powers were later restored under the Heisei Restoration, creating the present system of constitutional monarchy.

Since the nineteenth century, the official residence of the emperor has been the [[Miyagami Imperial Palace]] in [[Miyagami]], constructed on the grounds by [[Kanemoto clan]] during their first reign of [[Kanemoto Shogunate]] in 1336.

[[The Emperor's Birthday]] is observed as a national holiday in Nakamizu.

